#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228859 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e829ae4a-7612-5e40-8214-aa56027c38be" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating Under the Integral Sign"}] :question/id #uuid "301e7fe6-6892-5e0a-9368-b3857ae8fa39" :question/math-academy-id "e-21649" :question/problem "Given that $\\displaystyle J(t)=\\int_{0}^{1} (\\cos t+2x)^2\\, \\textrm{d}x$, calculate $J'(t)$ for $t\\in\\mathbb R$ by differentiating under the integral sign." :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on\n\n$$\nR = [a,b]\\times [c,d]\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}\n$$\n\nexists and is continuous on $R$. Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t) = \\int_{a}^b f(x,t)\\,\\textrm{d} x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d)$, and\n\n$$\nF'(t) = \\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t}\\int_{a}^b f(x,t)\\,\\textrm{d} x = \\int_{a}^b \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}(x,t)\\,\\textrm{d} x\n$$\n\nThis is known as differentiating under the integral sign.\n\nWe wish to compute $J'(t)$, where\n\n$$\nJ(t)=\\int_{0}^{1} (\\cos t+2x)^2\\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nDifferentiating under the integral sign, we get\n\n$$\nJ'(t) = \\int_{0}^{1} \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial t}\\Bigl[(\\cos t+2x)^2\\Bigr]\\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nNext, let's compute the partial derivative with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(\\cos t + 2x)^{2}] &= 2(\\cos t + 2x) \\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}(\\cos t + 2x) \\\\\n&= 2(\\cos t + 2x) \\cdot (-\\sin t) \\\\\n&=-2(\\cos t + 2x)\\sin t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this back into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nJ^{′}(t) &= ∫_{0}^{1} - 2(\\cos t + 2x)\\sin tdx \\\\\n&=-2\\sin t∫_{0}^{1}(\\cos t + 2x)dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the remaining integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{0}^{1}(\\cos t + 2x)dx &= \\cos t \\cdot x \\mid _{0}^{1} + x^{2} \\mid _{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= \\cos t + 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nJ'(t) =-2\\sin t\\,(\\cos t+1)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228874 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbefb3bb-4783-5d77-b404-1f43ee8765de" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Cases Where Differentiating Under the Integral Sign Applies"}] :question/id #uuid "830924ac-a51c-505a-b5b4-c8a4ad916dfe" :question/math-academy-id "e-21732" :question/problem "For which of the following functions is it valid to differentiate under the integral sign?\n\n1. $\\displaystyle F_1(t) = \\int_0^1 t\\cos x\\,\\textrm{d} x, \\quad t \\in \\mathbb R$\n2. $\\displaystyle F_2(t) = \\int_0^1 \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{\\mid x - t \\mid}}\\,\\textrm{d} x, \\quad t \\in (0,1)$\n3. $\\displaystyle F_3(t) = \\int_0^1 \\dfrac{1}{1 + (x - t)^2}\\,\\textrm{d} x, \\quad t \\in [0,1]$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on\n\n$$\nR = [a,b]\\times [c,d]\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}\n$$\n\nexists and is continuous on $R$. Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t) = \\int_{a}^b f(x,t)\\,\\textrm{d} x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d)$, and\n\n$$\nF'(t) = \\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t}\\int_{a}^b f(x,t)\\,\\textrm{d} x = \\int_{a}^b \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}(x,t)\\,\\textrm{d} x\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have\n$f_1(x,t) = t\\cos x$.\nWe fix $t_0 \\in \\mathbb R$ and choose $c < t_0 < d$ such that $[c,d] \\subseteq \\mathbb R$, so $R = [0,1] \\times [c,d]$.Note that $f_1$ is continuous on $R$. Computing the partial derivative, we have\n$\\dfrac{\\partial f_1}{\\partial t} = \\cos x$\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R$. Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at $t_0$. And since our choice of $t_0$ was arbitrary, we can compute $F'_1(t)$ for all real $t$.\n- For the second function, we have\n$f_2(x,t) = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{\\mid x - t \\mid}}$.\nWe fix $t_0 \\in (0,1)$ and choose $c < t_0 < d$ such that $[c,d] \\subseteq (0,1)$, so $R = [0,1] \\times [c,d]$.Note that $f_2$ is undefined when $x = t$, since the denominator vanishes. Thus, $f_2$ is not continuous on $R$, and differentiating under the integral sign is *not* valid.\n- For the third function, we have\n$f_3(x,t) = \\dfrac{1}{1 + (x - t)^2}$.\nWe fix $t_0 \\in (0,1)$ and choose $c < t_0 < d$ such that $[c,d] \\subseteq [0,1]$, so $R = [0,1] \\times [c,d]$.Note that $f_3$ is continuous on $R$. Computing the partial derivative, we have\n$\\dfrac{\\partial f_3}{\\partial t} = \\dfrac{2(x - t)}{(1 + (x - t)^2)^2}$\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R$. Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at $t_0$. And since our choice of $t_0$ was arbitrary, we can compute $F'_3(t)$ for all $t \\in [0,1]$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229605 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e829ae4a-7612-5e40-8214-aa56027c38be" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating Under the Integral Sign"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "958f7ba9-45b9-5cf0-95b5-ea32ae4d67d0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228085}] :question/id #uuid "635c83de-1981-509e-9e67-8590851bc8fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-332944" :question/problem "Given that $I(t) = ∫_{0}^{1}(2x + t^{3})^{2}dx$, calculate $I^{′}(t)$ for $t ∈ R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.$I^{′}(t) =$\n{{field-1}}"}]}
